draw cache, fast opaque hole, cached text shaping: the pixels a scroll redraws without change

Measured on Instagram's feed scroll with MACAPK_DRAW_SAMPLER=1 (a daemon thread
that samples the drawing thread's stack every millisecond during rootView.draw):
~40% of the draw went to avatars filled with a BitmapShader and photos drawn
scaled, 12% to the SurfaceView/TextureView hole (DST_OUT through Java2D's
general loop), 10% to re-shaping every string with HarfBuzz.
- Canvas DRAW_CACHE: a scaled drawBitmap (Rect, Matrix, or (x, y) under a
  scaling CTM) and an ellipse/rounded-rect/rect fill with a BitmapShader are
  kept as device pixels, keyed by the bitmap and its generation id, the source
  rect or shader matrix and tile modes, the shape, filter/AA, and the sub-pixel
  phase; later frames copy them 1:1 under their own clip and composite. Admitted
  only the second time a draw is seen identical (first-sight admission churned
  ~700 MB a minute and GC stalled frames for seconds); sightings are remembered
  weakly per source object, so a noted bitmap is never kept alive. A cache of
  plain AA shapes (solid/gradient ovals, arcs) was measured and dropped: the key
  cost more than drawing them. Bounded LRU
  (MACAPK_DRAW_CACHE_MB, 24), at most 1.2 MP per image. MACAPK_DRAW_CACHE=0 off.
- Canvas PUNCH_FAST: an opaque rectangular hole on whole device pixels under a
  rectangular clip is written as row clears (the DST_OUT fill's exact result).
  MACAPK_PUNCH_FAST=0 off.
- MacApkFont QUANTIZE_CACHE: the shaped, quantized GlyphVector is kept per
  thread by (font, text, liga); the letter-spacing path, which moves glyphs,
  uses quantizeUncached. MACAPK_QUANTIZE_CACHE=0 off.
A/B on the same scroll script: draw-only frames 5.16 -> 2.69 ms, full frames
7.67 -> 5.82 ms, 354 -> 1646 frames produced; the owner's profile screen
differs in 0.26% of pixels, none by more than 24/255.

